CH = dict(num=10, tag="Geography · Ch 5", theme="Minerals, their distribution, conventional and non-conventional energy",
 title="Minerals and Energy Resources",
 intro="Minerals are the building blocks of industry and energy resources run it. This chapter explains what minerals are, where India's iron ore, manganese, copper, bauxite, mica and others occur, and how coal, petroleum, gas, electricity and non-conventional sources meet India's energy needs, together with the need to conserve them.",
 big_questions=["What are minerals and how do they occur?", "Where are India's important minerals found?", "What are conventional and non-conventional energy sources?", "Why must minerals and energy be conserved?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "What Is a Mineral?"),
 ("p", "A mineral is a homogeneous, naturally occurring substance with a definable internal structure. Minerals are found in varied forms in nature, from the hardest diamond to the softest talc. They generally occur in **ores**, the accumulation of any mineral mixed with other elements."),
 ("ul", ["**Mode of occurrence:** in **igneous and metamorphic rocks** they are found in cracks, crevices, faults or joints (veins and lodes; e.g. tin, copper, zinc, lead); in **sedimentary rocks** in beds or layers, e.g. coal, iron ore, gypsum, potash salt, sodium salt; by **decomposition of surface rocks** in residual mass, e.g. bauxite; as **alluvial deposits** called placer deposits, e.g. gold, silver, tin, platinum; in **ocean waters**, e.g. common salt, magnesium, bromine; manganese nodules on the ocean floor.", "**Extraction:** mining (underground, deep, called shaft mining), open-cast or quarrying (cheapest), and drilling for petroleum and natural gas."]),
 ("h2", "Ferrous and Non-Ferrous Minerals"),
 ("table", ["Mineral", "Features", "Distribution in India"], [["**Iron ore**", "Basic mineral, backbone of industrial development; **magnetite** (best, over 70% iron, magnetic) and **haematite** (most important in quantity, about 60% iron)", "Odisha-Jharkhand belt (Badampahar, Gua), Durg-Bastar-Chandrapur belt in Chhattisgarh and Maharashtra (Bailadila exports to Japan and South Korea via Visakhapatnam), Ballari-Chitradurga-Chikkamagaluru-Tumakuru belt in Karnataka (Kudremukh), Maharashtra-Goa belt"], ["**Manganese**", "Used in steel-making and ferro-manganese alloys (about 10 kg for a tonne of steel); also bleaching powder, insecticides, paints", "Odisha is the leading producer"], ["**Copper**", "Malleable, ductile, a good conductor; used in electrical cables and electronics", "Balaghat (Madhya Pradesh), Khetri (Rajasthan), Singhbhum (Jharkhand)"], ["**Bauxite**", "Ore of aluminium; light, strong; clay-like; formed by decomposition of rocks", "Amarkantak plateau, Maikal hills, Bilaspur-Katni plateau; **Odisha** is the largest producer, Panchpatmali in Koraput"], ["**Mica**", "Transparent, black, green, red, yellow or brown; excellent insulator, used in electrical and electronic industries", "Koderma-Gaya-Hazaribagh belt in Jharkhand, Ajmer in Rajasthan, Nellore in Andhra Pradesh"], ["**Limestone**", "Found in sedimentary rocks of calcium carbonate; basic raw material for cement; also used in smelting iron ore", "Widely distributed"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "India has uneven distribution of minerals: about **97 per cent of coal** reserves occur in the Damodar, Sone, Mahanadi and Godavari valleys; **petroleum** reserves are in the Mumbai High, Gujarat and Assam; most metallic minerals lie in the old rocks of the Peninsular plateau, while limestone and other non-metallic minerals are found in sedimentary rocks on the western and eastern flanks."),
 ("h2", "Conventional Sources of Energy"),
 ("table", ["Source", "Notes"], [["Coal", "Most abundant fossil fuel in India; used for power generation and industry. Types by carbon content (from least to most): **peat** (low carbon, high moisture), **lignite** (low-grade brown coal, soft, high moisture; Neyveli, Tamil Nadu), **bituminous** (most popular in commercial use; metallurgical grade for blast furnace), **anthracite** (highest quality, hard). Gondwana coal (about 200 million years old) in Damodar valley (Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro), Godavari, Mahanadi, Sone; Tertiary coal (about 55 million years old) in north-eastern states"], ["Petroleum", "Provides fuel for heat and lighting, lubricants and raw material for industries; mostly found in anticlines and fault traps in sedimentary rocks. About 63 per cent of India's production is from **Mumbai High**, 18 per cent from Gujarat and 16 per cent from Assam. **Digboi** (Assam) is the oldest oil-producing area. Ankleshwar is the most important field in Gujarat. Refineries are ‘nodal industries’ for synthetic textiles, fertiliser and chemicals"], ["Natural gas", "Found with or without petroleum; used as fuel and as industrial raw material (CNG, PNG); large reserves in Krishna-Godavari basin; Jaisalmer, Mumbai High, Gulf of Khambhat; **HBJ pipeline** links Hazira (Gujarat), Bijaipur (Madhya Pradesh) and Jagdishpur (Uttar Pradesh)"], ["Electricity", "Generated by running water turning turbines (**hydro**) and by burning coal, petroleum or natural gas (**thermal**). Hydroelectricity: Bhakra-Nangal, Damodar Valley Corporation, Kopili Hydel project. Thermal power stations use coal, petroleum and natural gas"], ["Nuclear", "Uranium and thorium; Jharkhand and Aravalli ranges of Rajasthan; thorium in monazite sands of Kerala. Power stations: Tarapur (Maharashtra), Rawatbhata (Rajasthan), Kalpakkam (Tamil Nadu), Narora (Uttar Pradesh), Kaiga (Karnataka), Kakrapar (Gujarat)"]]),
 ("h2", "Non-Conventional Sources of Energy"),
 ("ul", ["**Solar energy:** photovoltaic technology converts sunlight directly into electricity; India is a tropical country with ample sunshine. The **Bhuj** (Gujarat) plant is an important solar plant. Solar energy is expected to replace kerosene and firewood in rural areas.", "**Wind energy:** India has potential in Gujarat, Rajasthan and Tamil Nadu; the largest wind farm cluster is from **Nagercoil to Madurai** in Tamil Nadu. India ranks high in the world in installed wind power capacity.", "**Biogas:** organic waste such as cattle dung, agricultural waste and sewage produces gas; ‘gobar gas plants’ provide energy for cooking and lighting in rural areas.", "**Tidal energy:** high and low tides turn turbines; sites are the Gulf of Khambhat, Gulf of Kutch and the Sunderbans delta. **Geothermal energy:** heat from the earth, at Parvati valley near Manikaran (Himachal Pradesh) and the Puga Valley in Ladakh."]),
 ("h2", "Conservation of Mineral and Energy Resources"),
 ("ul", ["Minerals take millions of years to form, but their consumption is rapid: **the rate of replenishment is infinitely small** compared with the present rate of consumption. Mineral resources are finite and non-renewable.", "Measures: use of minerals in a **planned and sustainable manner**; improved technologies to allow use of low-grade ores at low cost; **recycling** of metals and use of scrap metals and substitutes.", "Energy: use **public transport**, switch off electrical devices when not needed, use power-saving devices, and shift to non-conventional sources. The slogan: **‘Energy saved is energy produced’**."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is an ore?", "Accumulation of a mineral mixed with other elements."),
  ("Which is the best iron ore?", "Magnetite, with over 70 per cent iron."),
  ("Name the leading producer of manganese and bauxite.", "Odisha."),
  ("Where is lignite found?", "Neyveli in Tamil Nadu (also Rajasthan)."),
  ("Name two nuclear power stations.", "Tarapur and Kalpakkam."),
  ("What is a placer deposit?", "Mineral accumulated in sands of valley floors and hills, e.g. gold."),
  ("Why should energy be conserved?", "Fossil fuels are limited, and their use pollutes."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Minerals occur in **veins, beds, residual deposits, placers and sea water**.", "**Iron ore** (magnetite, haematite) is concentrated in Odisha-Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Karnataka; **Odisha** leads in manganese and bauxite.", "Coal ranks as **peat, lignite, bituminous and anthracite**; **Mumbai High** is the top oil field.", "Solar, wind, biogas, tidal and geothermal are non-conventional sources.", "**‘Energy saved is energy produced’**: conserve through efficiency, recycling and shift to renewables."])
